{% extends "base.html.j2" %}
{% block title %}How It Works | ISDS Thematic Watcher{% endblock %}
{% block description %}The whole instrument on one chart, drawn deterministically from the repository it describes: the {{ sources | length }} sources it reads &mdash; {{ sources_open | length }} open repositories and {{ sources_operator | length }} feeds inside the operator's own Google account, which nobody else can re-run &mdash; the automated pipeline, the multi-stage AI-assisted classification and review workflow, the operator's verification column, and every Monday deliverable.{% endblock %}

{% block content %}
<section class="page-head">
  <div class="measure">
    <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <ol>
        <li><a href="{{ root }}index.html">Home</a></li>
        <li aria-current="page">How It Works</li>
      </ol>
    </nav>
    <p class="page-kicker">The instrument</p>
    <h1 class="page-title">How the instrument works</h1>
    <p class="page-lede">
      The whole system on one chart, drawn directly from the repository it
      describes &mdash; every box and arrow cites the file that implements it.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="band">
  <div class="measure">
    <div class="doc-body">
      <p>
        Read it top to bottom. The first band is the {{ sources | length }} sources
        checked on every run. {{ sources_open | length | num_word | capitalize }} of them
        are open repositories &mdash; {{ sources_open | source_names }} &mdash; and
        {{ sources_operator | length | num_word }} are feeds inside Emory&rsquo;s own
        Google account: {{ sources_operator | source_names }}. That second group is a
        limit on what can be independently checked, not a detail of plumbing: Google
        generates those feeds for him, so a third party cannot re-run them and cannot
        audit what they did or did not deliver. The full catalogue, with what can be read
        from each source, is generated from the code at
        <a href="{{ repo_url }}/blob/main/analytics/source-inventory.md">analytics/source-inventory.md</a>.
        Beneath the band the work divides into four columns:
        <strong>the machine</strong> &mdash; the automated pipeline that collects,
        de-duplicates, scores, and quality-gates each candidate development;
        <strong>what gets sent</strong> &mdash; the emails and site updates each
        stage produces; <strong>the AI-assisted review workflow</strong> &mdash; a
        multi-stage classification and review process in which separate model
        passes, each running under different instructions and each box naming the
        model it runs on, screen, analyse, challenge, and edit the week&rsquo;s
        output, and in which a verification pass can block publication; and
        <strong>Emory&rsquo;s verification column</strong> &mdash; the checks the
        operator carries out by hand. The moving dots trace work flowing through
        the system. On a desktop browser, hovering over any box shows its full
        description and the exact code behind it.
      </p>
      <p class="caveat">
        <strong>Nothing produced here is currently being sent to a reader.</strong>
        While the instrument is under status-only validation, the weekly digest is
        delivered to the operator alone: the recipient list was narrowed by hand to a
        single address, and delivery to the researcher this instrument is built for is
        paused until the classifier has been validated. The archive on this site is
        still written every week and is still public. The pause is deliberate &mdash;
        publishing leads to a reader while the instrument that produced them is
        unvalidated is the thing the gate exists to prevent &mdash; and it is stated
        here because a page describing weekly deliverables would otherwise imply they
        are arriving somewhere.
      </p>
      <p class="caveat">
        <strong>These passes are not independent researchers.</strong> They share
        source material, prompt lineage, model family, and this project&rsquo;s
        framing assumptions, and can therefore share the same errors. Agreement
        between passes is not corroboration.
      </p>
      <p class="caveat">
        <strong>Claims here are machine-assisted and remain provisional</strong>
        unless an item is individually marked human-verified.
        {% if review.cycles_completed and review.claims_tracked %}
        Two counts describe that review and they are not the same count.
        The repository&rsquo;s review log records {{ review.cycles_completed }}
        completed operator review
        cycle{{ '' if review.cycles_completed == 1 else 's' }}, the most recent on
        {{ review.cycle_last }} &mdash; each a sit-down pass over a sample of the
        record, written up in full. Separately, of the
        {{ review.claims_tracked }} claims the append-only verification ledger tracks,
        <strong>{{ review.claims_marked }} are recorded in it as
        operator-verified</strong> against a named source; the other
        {{ review.claims_tracked - review.claims_marked }} carry no mark.
        That second count needs reading carefully.
        {% if review.mark_days == 1 %}
        All {{ review.claims_marked }} marks were entered on a single day,
        {{ review.mark_last }}, in one batch &mdash; so that date records when a mark
        was written down, not when a claim was verified, and one of the notes records a
        verification the operator gave in chat on an earlier date.
        {% else %}
        The marks were entered between {{ review.mark_first }} and
        {{ review.mark_last }}, and those are the dates a mark was written down rather
        than the dates a claim was verified.
        {% endif %}
        Most were entered in session under the operator&rsquo;s standing instruction
        that a verification given in chat is a mark, and the ledger notes say so; each
        of those is an operator verification of that claim, and none of them is a
        review cycle of its own.
        {% if review.marks_self_checked %}
        But {{ review.marks_self_checked }} of the {{ review.claims_marked }} record a
        primary source the assistant fetched and checked against itself rather than one
        the operator supplied or read. The ledger marks those operator-verified too, and
        it is append-only, so the count above restates what the ledger says rather than
        narrowing it here &mdash; and this sentence is how you know the difference.
        {% endif %}
        Both counts are read from the record at build time,
        not written here. These are research-record claims &mdash; the assertions
        behind the memos and one-pagers &mdash; and not digest entries, which are
        counted separately.
        {% else %}
        Human review of those claims is recorded in the repository&rsquo;s review log
        and verification ledger; this page states no figure it cannot read from them.
        {% endif %}
        {% if digest_verified == 0 %}
        No digest entry &mdash; and no sentence on this website resting on one &mdash;
        has been checked by a human against its original source; every archived
        digest entry to date is machine-screened only.
        {% else %}
        Of the {{ digest_total }} distinct entries published across the archived
        digests, <strong>{{ digest_verified }} {{ 'has' if digest_verified == 1 else 'have' }}
        been opened by the operator and checked against the original source</strong>{% if digest_rejected %},
        and {{ digest_rejected }} {{ 'was' if digest_rejected == 1 else 'were' }} rejected as
        unsupported by {{ 'its' if digest_rejected == 1 else 'their' }} source{% endif %};
        the remaining {{ digest_total - digest_verified - digest_rejected }} are
        machine-screened only. Verified entries carry a signed, append-only ledger
        event; the count above is read from that ledger at build time, not asserted
        here.
        {% endif %}
        The operator has no access
        to paywalled sources, so claims resting on them cannot be operator-verified
        at all.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <figure class="workflow-figure">
    <div class="workflow-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Workflow chart; scrolls horizontally on small screens" tabindex="0">
      {{ workflow_svg | safe }}
    </div>
    <figcaption>
      Drawn deterministically from the repository&rsquo;s own workflow description;
      a build-time check verifies that the drawing matches that description. The
      description is maintained by hand and is not itself generated from the code.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>
{% endblock %}
